        "reason-1": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "1",
            "title": "Your baby's bottle may be shedding millions of microplastics into every feed",
            "body": "<p>In 2020, researchers at Trinity College Dublin published a study in Nature Food on the plastic most baby bottles are made from: polypropylene. Following the standard WHO steps for preparing formula, they measured <strong>up to 16 million microplastic particles per liter</strong> from a single bottle, plus trillions of even smaller nanoplastics. Their estimate for a bottle-fed infant: <strong>more than a million particles swallowed every day</strong>. Polypropylene makes up roughly 82% of the bottles sold worldwide, which means most parents are doing this without ever being told.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image-2": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "https://mokababy.co/cdn/shop/t/12/assets/moka-listicle-bloodstream-particles-4x3.jpg?v=181696134951427253691783477672",
            "alt": "Microscopic particles moving among red blood cells",
            "caption": "Illustration of microscopic particles moving among red blood cells"
          }
        },
        "reason-2": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "2",
            "title": "Microscopic doesn't mean harmless. It means it gets in everywhere.",
            "body": "<p>Most parents are never told the scary part: these particles don't just pass through. They're <strong>small enough to cross from the gut into the bloodstream</strong>, and they've already shown up in <strong>human blood, breast milk, and babies before they're even born</strong>. Once they're in, an infant can't flush them out the way an adult can, so they build up during the most fragile months of development. Infant research keeps linking that buildup to inflammation and hormone disruption, right when your baby's brain, organs and hormones are still forming. Small was never the same as safe.</p>"
          }
        },
        "image-3": {
          "type": "image",
          "settings": {
            "image": "https://mokababy.co/cdn/shop/t/12/assets/moka-listicle-scratched-glass.jpg?v=44791940780741439941783477681",
            "alt": "Bottle sterilizing and scratched plastic placeholder",
            "caption": "Macro view of scratches inside a repeatedly washed glass bottle"
          }
        },
        "reason-3": {
          "type": "reason",
          "settings": {
            "number": "3",
            "title": "It isn't a one-time thing. It gets worse with every wash and sterilize.",
            "body": "<p>The same study found the problem compounds. <strong>Heat and agitation are what break the plastic down</strong>, and that's exactly what every feed involves: hot water, sterilizing, shaking and scrubbing. Each cycle scratches the surface and releases more. Raise the water from the recommended 158°F to near-boiling, and particle release <strong>jumped from about 6 million to 55 million per liter</strong>. The bottle you sterilize to keep clean is the same bottle shedding plastic because of it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "cta-3": {
          "type": "cta",
          "settings": {
            "label": "See How Moka Fixes It",
            "link": "https://mokababy.co/"
          }
        },
